    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Funan is generally considered the first known kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, though its own name is unknown: 'Funan' is Chinese, and its people may have been Khmer, Austronesian or a mixture of both.</li><li>Roman gold medallions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoninus-pius">Antoninus Pius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> and Persian, Indian and Greek goods found at Óc Eo show a trading state that may have been Ptolemy's Kattigara, where Roman and Chinese merchants met.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-shiman">Fan Shiman</a>, the 3rd-century '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a> of Funan', built great ships, attacked more than ten kingdoms and extended his territory five or six thousand li before dying on campaign against Chin-lin, the 'Frontier of Gold'.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A group of Funanese musicians visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 263 CE and so impressed the emperor that he ordered an institute for Funanese music to be founded near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

